#doc (Manual) "The CatCrypt verified compiler for cryptography" =>

🚧 *Under construction — experimental research prototype.* This compiler is part
of the CatCrypt project ([ePrint 2026/604](https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/604.pdf)),
developed over many months with the help of AI. Interfaces, the covered source
fragment, and the trusted base are still evolving; a full manual audit is ongoing.
It is not a production tool.

CatCrypt compiles cryptography from a high-level source to constant-time machine
code, entirely inside the Lean 4 proof assistant, with a machine-checked proof
that the emitted bytes compute the source specification. It takes two kinds of
source — Rust extracted by [hax](https://github.com/hacspec/hax), and relational
specifications written directly in Lean — and lowers each through a stack of typed
MLIR-style dialects to an owned backend that encodes instructions to bytes for
x86-64, AArch64, and RISC-V.

The code blocks below are illustrative transcriptions. For the full
per-declaration API, see the [API reference](api/index.html), generated by
doc-gen4 and served alongside this manual.

# What the compiler guarantees

One *source-to-assembly* theorem governs the pipeline. For a source in the covered
fragment, the emitted bytes **denote the source specification** (functional
correctness), run in **constant time**, and **preserve the source protocol's
security** against target-level attackers (a robust hyperproperty, by
back-translation of target contexts). A single denotation-preserving encoding
relation drives three uses at once: the equality-saturation optimizer, the
relational compiler, and the transport that carries a protocol's security bound
down to the emitted code. Optimizing, compiling, and preserving security are three
uses of one object.

Every dialect in the stack carries a Lean denotation, and every lowering is a Lean
function proved to preserve it. A well-typed term therefore denotes its
specification by construction.

# The pipeline

Two frontends feed one SSA `Com` intermediate representation:

- the **hax frontend** compiles extracted Rust in a structured fragment;
- the **relational frontend** compiles a Lean specification directly, in the style
  of Rupicola.

From `Com`, the source descends through per-family crypto dialects that mirror the
prime-IR MLIR op families — field, modular and Montgomery arithmetic, elliptic
curves, polynomial and NTT coefficient forms, pairing — and a word-level Jasmin
dialect, with an equality-saturation optimizer applied along the way. The owned
backend then encodes the result to bytes for the three architectures. The `Com`
framework follows [lean-mlir](https://github.com/opencompl/lean-mlir) for its
structure; code generation is the compiler's own and does not depend on it.

# Scope

The hax frontend compiles a structured fragment of extracted Rust — A-normal form
with structured control flow (`IsCoveredCtl`), free nesting of loops, conditionals,
guarded loops, and bounded `while`, now including SIMD extension operations. It is
not a proof that arbitrary Rust compiles; the fragment widens as the frontend
grows. The relational frontend compiles Lean specifications directly.

Security composes to the emitted bytes end to end. Four chains carry a protocol's
security through the whole pipeline to its byte-level interface with an axiom-clean
ledger — Ed25519/EdDSA, Schnorr, X25519, and ECDSA (for Ed25519, the standard
forgery bound reaches the 32-byte wire interface). A wider set of roughly a hundred
and thirty protocols is tied to the emitted code at the realization level.

# The backends and the trusted base

The compiler emits one owned in-Lean backend to three architectures. The **x86-64**
backend is the benchmarked target and carries the constant-time clause. The
**AArch64** and **RISC-V** backends emit and run end to end with proved byte
encodings; their functional correctness is proved, while the authoritative
constant-time model is currently x86-only. For **RISC-V**, the emitted scalar
instruction semantics are proved equal to the official
[Sail](https://github.com/riscv/sail-riscv) specification.

Naming what is assumed rather than proved: the per-architecture machine model the
backend is checked against, and — for a security claim — the protocol's
cryptographic-hardness assumption. The byte-level instruction facts
(`decode ∘ encode`, the ADX carry-chain instructions) are proved. One `native_decide`
reflection axiom discharges an AES S-box array-size check. `jasminc` serves as a
validation backend and remains on a small, tagged residual set of benchmark rows.

# Performance

The security and constant-time guarantee comes at same-tier cost, not as a
separate performance contribution. Measured against the fastest deployed
constant-time references on one host, the emitted assembly matches hand-tuned
verified references on the field and bignum kernels — Curve25519 field
multiplication at 0.98× s2n-bignum, `bignum_mul_4_8` at 1.05× s2n and 0.982× the
Jasmin reference, `mpn` basecase multiplication faster than libgmp at every size —
and stays within a small same-tier factor across the roster. Where the compiler
adopts a verified SIMD or field leaf, the ratio is ~1.0× by construction; where it
emits an unadopted kernel, the scheduling tax is at most a small factor (an in-Lean
X25519 ladder at 1.61× s2n marks that modularity floor).

# How this documentation is organized

Three layers document the compiler, read together: this **manual** (the narrative
and worked examples), the **API reference** (every declaration with its signature,
generated by doc-gen4), and the **proof blueprint** (the theorem-dependency graph
linking each informal statement to its formal theorem). Reproduce the axiom ledger
of the end-to-end results with `lake env lean AxiomAudit.lean`.
